                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "can_could",
                    "title": "can / can't",
                    "explanation": "Can expresa habilidad (I can play football = sé jugar al fútbol) y también permiso o petición (Can I be the goalkeeper? = ¿puedo…?).\n\nEs igual para todas las personas (he can, nunca «he cans») y va seguido del verbo sin to: She can swim (no «She can to swim»).\n\nNegativa: can't (cannot). Pregunta: Can you…? — Yes, I can / No, I can't.\n\nPronunciación: en afirmativa can suena débil, /kən/; can't suena fuerte, /kɑːnt/.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Afirmativa",
                          "Lucía can play football."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "Sam can't kick the ball very well."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Pregunta",
                          "Can you play football?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Respuesta corta",
                          "Yes, I can. / No, I can't."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Permiso",
                          "Can we stop now?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Petición",
                          "Can you help me?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Can you play football, Lucía? — Yes, I can!",
                      "I can't run very fast.",
                      "Can I be the goalkeeper?",
                      "Who can bring some water? — I can."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "can_could",
                    "title": "could / couldn't · very and really",
                    "explanation": "Could es el pasado de can para la habilidad: I could swim when I was six (sabía nadar). Negativa: couldn't. Pregunta: Could you…?\n\nCould también sirve para pedir algo con más educación que can: Could you help me, please? Could I have some water?\n\nVery y really refuerzan un adjetivo o un adverbio: very fast, really good, really well. Van delante de la palabra que refuerzan. Not very suaviza: I can't run very fast (no corro muy rápido).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo",
                        "En español"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Habilidad en pasado",
                          "I could swim when I was six.",
                          "Sabía nadar a los seis años."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "I couldn't play football at school.",
                          "No sabía jugar al fútbol en el colegio."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Petición educada",
                          "Could you help me, please?",
                          "¿Podrías ayudarme, por favor?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "very / really",
                          "You can play really well.",
                          "Juegas muy bien."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I could play football when I was eight.",
                      "Sam couldn't kick the ball.",
                      "Could you bring some water, please?",
                      "Lucía can't run very fast, but she plays really well."
                    ]
                  }
                },
